A||||10||aji||New guy here with questions.||||1||
Z||000000||aji||02-21-2000||10:34 AM||||Hi there,<P>I am new here and wish to purchase a Fiero as a project car.  I have reviewed quite a bit of the forum and wish some advice.<P>1.  I am interested in an 87 or 88 GT.  Based on the info from you guys, there are only a few differences in the two years.  <BR>Could you help me here and list them for me.  <P>2.  I prefer a manual trans.  It is my observation that the best on suited for high power ouput would be the Getrag.  Am I correct?<P>3.  Do you guys think that I can pay standard retail recommended by Kelly Blue book, or do Fieros go for more because of popularity and the fact that they don't make them any more?<BR> <BR>4.  I am shooting for some real h.p. with an engine swap, can you fit wider wheels in the back than stock?  Can I have some custom ones made to give me at least 9.5" width?<P>5.  I want to go with a forced air 3800 or a V8.  I understand that the V8 has some issures with handling even after the rear sway bar.  This is important to me as I have an f-body and hate the handling.  I hope that the fiero handling can be improved somewhat.<P>6.  Since the weight is in the back, do the Fieros tend to be oeversteer happy.  I owned a Fiat X19 an the oversteer wasn't that bad.  This was also a nid-engine car.<P>Thanks..that is all I can think of for now.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||02-21-2000||01:08 PM||||Big differences between '87 and '88.  In '88, Pontiac completely changed the suspension.  It's better, but you can make the '87 suspension better than the stock '88 by adding/changing parts.<BR>The only stick you can get in an '87 or '88 GT is the Getrag 5-spd.  The auto was an option, but I hear the stick is alot more fun.<BR>You might pay more than Kellys, or you might pay less.  It's hard to say.  It depends alot on your area.  We've had this discussion here before.  There could be as much as $2000 price difference on the same car.<BR>I've never had a problem with oversteer but I know that some have.  Actually the weight distribution is closer to 50/50 than you think.  Something like 42/58?||||reg||1
Z||000002||aji||02-21-2000||03:03 PM||||Thanks for the info.  One of the cars that I am looking at is an 87.  After I was told about the improved suspension in the 88s I thought that I would wait.  the present owner has added a sway bar to the back and Bilstein shocks and possibly springs.  The car is low mileage and he is asking $5,500.  Maybe this would be good even though it still doesn't have some of the 88 suspension parts in it.  Could you possibly elaborate more?<P>Oh, he said that the paint was faded due to the sun down here.  So I guess it could use a paint job. <p>[This message has been edited by aji (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||02-21-2000||03:14 PM||||$5500 sounds high for an 87, especially if it needs paint. <P>If you are planning an engine swap anyway you are probably wasting money on a low mileage car. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||aji||02-21-2000||04:14 PM||||Agreed DRH,<P>If he won't come down, I will continue the search.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||02-21-2000||09:06 PM||||Aji, I have owned an 87 coupe, driven an 87 GT, and now have an 88formula. The difference in there handling characteristics is that the 88's ride better and have better handling characteristics than the 87. The 88's brakes are also almost fade proof and a whole lot better. They both are the same at their limits for stick thou. $5500 for that car is way too high...If you find an 88 for sale, GRAB IT.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||02-21-2000||09:36 PM||||How do you drive?  If you spend much time on freeways or go for top speed then you would want 5 spd. If you enjoy carving the twisties then a 4 spd maybe more to your liking.  Don't rule out the 86 GT. All the mods you do on the 87 can be done on the 86, and I have heard that the 5 speed is prone to some syncro failures. Can I get a witness? Anyway, I love my 86!||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||02-21-2000||11:23 PM||||IMHO, unless your stuck on the sound of the phrase "5 speed", a 4 great and cheaper to rebuild. Personally, I think the 4 speeds are faster.<P>Yes, the Getrag's are as bulletproof as paper. They are great trans, but not the cats-meow.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shiner||02-22-2000||01:31 AM||||Well, I have a synchro problem in my '87 GT's Getrag, 3rd gear to be exact.  The gearing is great for backroads hijinx.  I belive the getrag is lighter too, right guys?  It also really keeps those rev's down on the highway in 5th gear.  HOWEVER, I know getting this trans fixed is going to MUTILATE my wallet!  The availability of these trans' is severely limited, and I have seen them go for as high as $1200!  Anybody have an estimate on the cost of the synchro replacement?||||reg||1
Z||000009||dogtired||02-22-2000||02:37 AM||||Shimer, I have the same 87 third gear syncro grind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>I know Rodney Dickman has the procedure on his page on how to separate the case of the getrag, Is there anyone here that has installed the 3rd gear syncro??? Maybe can give us a little more info on how to do it???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Should one mess with all the Synchros? or just the one grinding??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Do ya think Shiners and Dogtireds can do such a procedure??? Or should we spend big bucks???    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000010||william_thorniii||02-22-2000||12:41 PM||||aji,<P>Sounds like you could be in the market for Super X's 88 at that price.  The Muncie 4-spd will allow slightly quicker acceleration due to the taller gearing.  If you really want some get off the line, then get the M19 from a 1984 Fiero.  That has the most aggressive gearing of all the Fiero's.  Also from the heresay that I have heard, the Muncie 4-spdit is more durable and more likely to survive under a heavy breathing engine than the Muncie/Getrag 5-spd.  <P><BR>Dogtired,<P>You can do anything that the shop can do, but it will take you much longer.  Of course by the second time (to fix what you did wrong the first time), it will go much quicker and you will do a better job   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>William T. Thorn, III<p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 02-22-2000).]||||reg||14
